    <h1>How to Play UNO Online</h1>
    <p class="page-subtitle">A step-by-step guide to starting your first game and playing well — from opening the page to calling that final UNO.</p>

    <p>If you have played UNO around a kitchen table, you already know the heart of it. Playing online just removes the setup: no shuffling, no dealing, no arguments about whose turn it is. This guide walks you through your very first online game from scratch, then covers the controls and a few tips that make the difference between playing and winning. The whole thing takes about a minute to learn.</p>

    <h2>Step 1: Enter a Nickname</h2>
    <p>Go to the <a href="/play">Play page</a> and type a nickname — this is what everyone else sees during the game. You can use up to 16 characters, and you do not need an account to do this. Signing in later is optional and only exists to save your stats and cosmetics across devices; you can play a hundred games without ever creating one.</p>

    <h2>Step 2: Create or Join a Room</h2>
    <p><strong>Create a Room:</strong> Click "Create New Room" to start a fresh game. You become the host, which means you control the rules and who is at the table, and you receive a short room code like <code>UNO-ABCD</code>. Flip on "Private Room" if you only want people with your link to join; leave it public and your room shows up in the browser for anyone looking for a game.</p>
    <p><strong>Join a Room:</strong> Enter a room code a friend shared with you, or click "Browse Public Rooms" to find open games you can jump straight into. If a game is already in progress, you can watch as a spectator and be seated automatically when the next round starts.</p>

    <h2>Step 3: Invite Your Friends</h2>
    <p>This is where online UNO shines. As the host, click "Copy Invite Link" and paste it into WhatsApp, Discord, a group text, or anywhere else — your friends tap it and land directly in your room, no code typing required. You can invite up to 19 other people, and they can join from any device. There is a one-tap WhatsApp share button too, for the fastest possible "get in here, we're starting."</p>

    <h2>Step 4: Choose Your Rules and Start</h2>
    <p>Before starting, the host picks a game mode. <strong>Classic</strong> follows the official rules. <strong>No Mercy</strong> turns on stacking, Wild +8 cards, and elimination for a brutal, fast game. <strong>Custom</strong> lets you toggle each house rule individually — stacking, Seven-Zero, Jump-In, turn timer length, and more. Not sure which to pick? The <a href="/game-modes">game modes guide</a> compares all three. Once at least two players have joined, the host clicks "Start Game." Everyone is dealt 7 cards and a card is flipped to begin the discard pile.</p>

    <h2>Step 5: Play Your Cards</h2>
    <p>On your turn, play a card that matches the top of the discard pile by <strong>color</strong>, <strong>number</strong>, or <strong>symbol</strong>. Tap or click the card you want to play. If you have nothing legal, click the draw pile to take a card — and if that card can be played, you may play it immediately, or pass. A visual timer shows how long you have; if it runs out, auto-play makes a legal move for you so the game never stalls.</p>
    <p>Keep an eye on the special cards, because they are where UNO gets its bite: Skip loses the next player their turn, Reverse flips the direction of play, Draw Two forces the next player to draw two cards, and the Wild and Wild Draw Four let you change the active color (with the +4 also forcing four cards on your opponent). Our full <a href="/rules">rules guide</a> explains every card in detail.</p>

    <h2>Step 6: Call UNO!</h2>
    <p>The moment you play your second-to-last card and have just one card left, press the UNO button — fast. There is a short grace window, and if another player catches you before you call it, you draw two penalty cards. Forgetting to call UNO is the most common way players hand away a winning position, so make it a reflex: play the card and call UNO in the same motion.</p>

    <h2>Step 7: Win the Round</h2>
    <p>The first player to get rid of all their cards wins. The game shows the final standings, awards coins and XP, and the host can start a new round instantly. If you turned on the Play for Places rule, the round keeps going after the first player goes out so everyone earns a final rank — great for big groups.</p>

    <h2>Handy Controls and Features</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reconnection:</strong> Drop off Wi-Fi or close the tab by accident? Rejoin and your hand is exactly where you left it.</li>
      <li><strong>Emoji reactions:</strong> Tap the reaction button to celebrate a win or groan at a Draw Four — there is no open chat, so it stays family-friendly.</li>
      <li><strong>Card styles:</strong> Switch the look of your cards from the card-style button; it is a personal preference only you see.</li>
      <li><strong>Sound and fullscreen:</strong> Toggle game sounds, and go fullscreen (or landscape on mobile) for the most comfortable view.</li>
      <li><strong>Spectator mode:</strong> Join a game already in progress to watch until the next round deals you in.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tips for New Players</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Play your high number cards early — they are the hardest to get rid of at the end.</li>
      <li>Save your Wild and especially your Wild Draw Four for when the player after you is close to winning.</li>
      <li>Watch which colors are piling up so you do not get stuck holding a dead color.</li>
      <li>Aim your Skips and Draw Twos at the player with the fewest cards, not at random.</li>
      <li>Never, ever forget to call UNO — the penalty has ended more winning hands than any card.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>When you are ready to go from "knows the rules" to "keeps winning," our blog has a full breakdown of <a href="/blog/how-to-win-at-uno">twelve strategies that actually work</a>.</p>

